<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The privacy manifest.

  Apple requires this to declare tracking, collected data, and any use of the APIs it calls
  "required reason". For most apps it is a chore. Here it is the machine readable form of
  the claim the README leads with, so it is worth reading rather than copying:

  - No tracking, and no tracking domains.
  - No collected data types at all. Not "collected but not linked to you", not "collected
    for app functionality". None, because nothing leaves the device except the iCloud
    Keychain sync the user turns on, which is Apple's transport and not this app's
    collection.
  - Two required reason APIs, and no others. UserDefaults, for this app's own preferences:
    sort order, appearance, chosen icon, whether sync is on, whether the lock is on and its
    grace period. Never a secret, never an account name. See
    OpenFactor/Settings/Preferences.swift, which says the same thing in code. And file
    timestamps, for one line in the share extension's inbox, explained at its entry below.

  The other categories were checked against Apple's list rather than assumed, and are
  genuinely absent: no system boot time, no disk space, no active keyboard. The share
  extension declares nothing of its own because it only writes to the inbox; reading a
  timestamp happens in the app.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<!-- CA92.1: access to information from the app itself, and app group
				     containers it is a member of. This app reads and writes only its own
				     preferences. -->
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<!-- C617.1: timestamps of files inside the app container, an app group
				     container, or the app's CloudKit container.

				     One call site, SharedInbox.pending() in OpenFactorCore, which reads
				     .modificationDate to sort what the share extension left newest first
				     and to sweep anything older than the freshness window unread. The
				     inbox is in the app group container, so this reason is the case
				     verbatim rather than the nearest fit.

				     This entry was missing from the day the freshness window landed. It
				     is invisible to the build and surfaces as an automated notice at
				     upload, so ci.yml now fails when a file timestamp API appears in
				     source without this declaration. Verified against Apple's published
				     list rather than a summary of it: modificationDate is named in the
				     file timestamp category, and C617.1 is the app group container
				     reason. The neighbouring codes are not close calls, they are
				     different cases: DDA9.1 is showing a timestamp to the person, and
				     3B52.1 is a file they picked themselves. -->
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
